# Prisma Conventions

## Schema

### Naming

- Model names: PascalCase (`User`, `OrderItem`) — Prisma default, no `@@map` needed
- Column names: camelCase (`firstName`, `createdAt`) — Prisma default, no `@map` needed
- Exception: OAuth/external-spec fields stay snake_case as-is (`refresh_token`, `access_token`, `id_token`)

### Primary Keys

- Use `Int @id @default(autoincrement())` on every model
- Add a `hashId String @unique` column for public-facing IDs
- Generate hashId in the service layer using `sqids` after creation
- API routes and responses use `hashId` only — never expose the integer ID

### Standard Fields

- Always add `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `deletedAt` (soft delete) to every model
- Add `@@index` for any column used in WHERE or ORDER BY
- Every relation must have explicit `onDelete` behavior (`Cascade`, `SetNull`, `Restrict`)

## Query Patterns

- Use `$transaction` for multi-table writes
- Soft delete: `findFirst` + `where: { deletedAt: null }` (or use middleware)
- Pagination always returns `{ data, meta: { total, page, limit, totalPages, hasNextPage, hasPreviousPage } }`
- Never use `findMany` without `take` — unbounded queries are a production incident
- Use `select` or response DTOs to avoid leaking sensitive fields (passwords, tokens, integer IDs)

## PrismaService

- Wrap `PrismaClient` in a NestJS service (`PrismaService extends PrismaClient`)
- Register in a `@Global()` `PrismaModule`
- Implement `onModuleInit` (connect) and `onModuleDestroy` (disconnect)
- Enable query logging in development only
